%U A400124 53098038031569089586,5287998728866770252463,567465791371462442228266,65282969928084747839600449,8015519204370116693068188906
%N A400124 Number of triples (A, S, R), where A is an acyclic digraph on n labeled nodes and a distinguished node r, S and R are subsets of V(A), r has no out-edge in A and belongs to R, such that adding a new node h with edges h -> S and R -> h gives a strongly connected digraph D reducible as a flow graph from every node, r has exactly one in-edge in D, and D - r is strongly connected.
%C A400124 The 'flat-plus-one-return' core: Phi(x) = Sum a(n) x^n/n! is the substitution kernel of the everywhere-reducible block series, C = x*Phi(C) (see sese_all_valid_blocks in the linked program of A400123), so this sequence closes the everywhere-reducible family to all orders.
%C A400124 Return-attachment Theorem: r hangs either from h (always admissible, giving the flat_everywhere_reducible count F(n)) or from a node u of A, which is admissible iff every dominance cone on u's dominator chain has all its exits pointing straight back at h ('focus UP').
%D A400124 M. S. Hecht and J. D. Ullman, Flow graph reducibility, SIAM J. Comput. 1 (1972), 188-202.
%D A400124 M. S. Hecht and J. D. Ullman, Characterizations of reducible flow graphs, J. ACM 21 (1974), 367-375.
%D A400124 R. W. Robinson, Counting digraphs with restrictions on the strong components, in T.-H. Ku (ed.), Combinatorics and Graph Theory '95, World Scientific, Singapore, 1995, pp. 343-354.
%D A400124 R. E. Tarjan, Testing flow graph reducibility, J. Comput. System Sci. 9 (1974), 355-365.
%H A400124 Gregory Morse, <a href="/A400124/b400124.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..99</a>
%F A400124 Let tau = tau(x) be the unique formal power series with tau(0) = 0 satisfying tau = x*(2*exp(tau)-1)*(2*exp(tau)-1-tau).
%F A400124 E.g.f.: Phi(x) = Sum_{n>=0} a(n)*x^n/n! = exp(tau) + tau*exp(tau)*(2*exp(tau)-1)/(2*exp(tau)-1-2*tau*exp(tau)); thus a(n) = n!*[x^n] Phi(x).
%F A400124 To expand through degree N, iterate the defining equation N times starting from tau = 0, truncating modulo x^(N+1). Then substitute in Phi; its denominator has constant term 1.
%o A400124 (Python) everywhere_reducible_sequence("flat_plus_one_return", 19) # See Python link in A400123 for program code.
